# React TypeScript Patterns

## `useRef` requires an initial value

React 19 types require exactly 1 argument for `useRef`. Omitting the initial value causes a build error.

```typescript
// BAD — missing initial value
const ref = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>>()

// GOOD — pass null and include null in the type union
const ref = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null)
```

**Mutable vs readonly:** If you need to reassign `.current` (e.g. storing a timeout ID), include `| null` in the type parameter. Without it, React 19 treats `.current` as `readonly`.

## `clearTimeout` / `clearInterval` don't accept `null`

When a ref includes `| null`, guard before calling `clearTimeout`:

```typescript
// BAD — clearTimeout doesn't accept null
clearTimeout(debounceRef.current)

// GOOD — guard first
if (debounceRef.current) clearTimeout(debounceRef.current)
```

## Discriminated unions: narrow before accessing variant-specific properties

When iterating over a filtered subset of a union type, type the variable with the specific variant so TypeScript knows which properties exist:

```typescript
type SearchResult =
  | { type: 'deck'; id: string; name: string }
  | { type: 'card'; name: string }

// BAD — typed as full union, no access to .id
const decks: SearchResult[] = results.filter(...)

// GOOD — narrowed to the specific variant
const decks: Extract<SearchResult, { type: 'deck' }>[] = results.filter(...)
```
